{"id":"n-20261023-ufsv7f","category":"health","display_title":"ADHD Treatment Crisis in Northern Ireland","display_title_he":"משבר הטיפול בהפרעות קשב וריכוז בצפון אירלנד","short_summary":"Adults with ADHD in Northern Ireland face long waiting lists and a lack of publicly funded services, forcing many to pay thousands of pounds annually for private healthcare.","english_level":"C1","words":432,"reading_time":3,"image":null,"sources":[{"name":"BBC News","domain":"bbc.co.uk","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cvze4wg10389o","title":"'Wildly unfair that my husband and I pay £4k a year for ADHD treatment'","published_at":"2026-10-23T05:14:41.000Z"}],"published_at":"2026-10-23T05:14:41.000Z","first_published_in_app_at":"2026-10-23T06:30:26.047Z","quality_score":9,"interest_score":8,"verification_status":"verified","content_status":"published","paragraphs":["Adults living with Attention Deficit Hyperactivity Disorder in Northern Ireland are urgently demanding action regarding a severe lack of publicly funded medical services. While other regions of the United Kingdom offer established care pathways, Northern Ireland remains without a formally commissioned, publicly funded adult ADHD service. Consequently, local health trusts must manage patient referrals within existing resources, creating extensive waiting lists and driving many individuals toward private healthcare providers.","Rebecca Bryson, an entrepreneur who owns three businesses, described the current situation as wildly unfair and inhumane. Because childhood diagnoses do not automatically transfer into adult support systems once patients turn eighteen, adults find themselves entirely on their own. Bryson and her husband, who both live with ADHD, spend approximately four thousand pounds every single year on private psychiatric reviews, prescriptions, and necessary medication. According to Bryson, while her multiple businesses allow her to cover these steep expenses, it represents an exorbitant amount of money that most ordinary people simply do not possess.","The systemic failures affect individuals across different generations. Twenty-eight-year-old Jordan Young explained that he struggled extensively to access local ADHD services because healthcare doors were constantly closed to new patients. Ultimately, he gained access to private assessments and follow-ups only because his grandmother utilized her private health insurance to help him. Young emphasized that the health minister must allocate a dedicated budget to treat adult ADHD with the seriousness it clearly warrants.","Medical professionals also face mounting administrative and clinical dilemmas. Dr Allen McCullough, a general practitioner with thirty-five years of experience, noted that managing diagnoses has evolved into a complex picture. Although he can technically refer patients to local adult services, he does so knowing that no clinician is available to receive them. Furthermore, Dr McCullough explained why some GPs hesitate to participate in shared-care arrangements with private practitioners. Without official notifications detailing the qualifications of the diagnosing clinician or complete clinical histories, primary care physicians cannot safely involve themselves in prescribing specialized medications.","Political figures have strongly criticized the governmental response. Alliance MLA Peter McReynolds argued that adults with ADHD have been completely forgotten by the Department of Health. Having pushed for a commissioned service since two thousand and twenty-four, McReynolds maintained that establishing a proper pathway would prove transformative for the local population and ultimately save public funds in the long term. In response, a Department of Health spokesperson stated that demand continuously exceeds available capacity, leading patients toward private alternatives. Officials maintained that any future commissioning decisions must be evaluated strictly within the context of severe funding constraints across the entire health and social care sector."],"facts":[{"fact":"Adults with ADHD in Northern Ireland are demanding urgent action over a lack of publicly funded services.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Northern Ireland lacks a formally commissioned, publicly funded adult ADHD pathway, unlike other parts of the UK.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Rebecca Bryson and her husband spend around £4,000 a year on private psychiatric reviews, prescriptions, and medication for their ADHD.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"The Department of Health acknowledges increasing demand and capacity issues, noting that decisions must consider overall affordability.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Dr Allen McCullough, a GP of 35 years, highlighted challenges with shared-care arrangements and unverified private diagnoses.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Jordan Young, 28, had to rely on his grandmother's private health insurance to access an ADHD diagnosis.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Alliance MLA Peter McReynolds stated that adults with ADHD have been forgotten by the Department of Health.","sources":[1]}],"glossary":[{"text":"Adults","lemma":"adult","pos":"noun","he":"מבוגרים","name":false},{"text":"Attention Deficit Hyperactivity Disorder","lemma":"attention deficit hyperactivity disorder","pos":"noun","he":"הפרעת קשב, ריכוז היפראקטיביות","name":false},{"text":"Northern Ireland","lemma":"northern ireland","pos":"name","he":"צפון אירלנד","name":true},{"text":"urgently","lemma":"urgently","pos":"adverb","he":"דחופה","name":false},{"text":"action","lemma":"action","pos":"noun","he":"פעולה","name":false},{"text":"severe","lemma":"severe","pos":"adjective","he":"חמור","name":false},{"text":"lack","lemma":"lack","pos":"noun","he":"מחסור","name":false},{"text":"publicly funded","lemma":"publicly funded","adjective":true,"pos":"adjective","he":"ממומן ציבורית","name":false},{"text":"medical services","lemma":"medical service","pos":"noun","he":"שירותים רפואיים","name":false},{"text":"regions","lemma":"region","pos":"no